WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.629
Two credits hit one account at once: plus 50 and plus 30.

2
00:00:06.629 --> 00:00:12.143
Each one reads the balance, converts with an FX call that suspends, then writes.

3
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:18.300
A common fix is to confine them to one thread, with a view like limitedParallelism(1).

4
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.335
Is that enough?

5
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:25.622
A reads 100 and suspends in the FX call.

6
00:00:25.622 --> 00:00:29.895
The thread is free, so B runs, and reads 100 too.

7
00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:31.029
A writes 150.

8
00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:33.907
Then B writes 130, right over it.

9
00:00:33.907 --> 00:00:35.564
A's credit is lost.

10
00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:39.155
One thread keeps others out only between suspensions.

11
00:00:39.155 --> 00:00:42.607
A suspension in the middle lets the next coroutine in.

12
00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:46.213
A Mutex holds across the suspension.

13
00:00:46.213 --> 00:00:50.459
A takes the lock, reads, and suspends still holding it.

14
00:00:50.459 --> 00:00:54.473
B asks for the lock and suspends, holding no thread.

15
00:00:54.473 --> 00:00:57.175
A writes 150 and releases the lock.

16
00:00:57.175 --> 00:00:59.723
Then B reads 150, and writes 180.

17
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:09.611
Or keep the view, and move the suspension out: convert first, then read and write with nothing in between.

18
00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:11.585
The two FX calls even overlap.

19
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:18.156
Code like this waits a lot, and real waits make tests slow and flaky.

20
00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:20.106
runTest swaps the clock.

21
00:01:20.106 --> 00:01:26.122
Delays on the test dispatcher are skipped, and a virtual clock jumps ahead instead.

22
00:01:26.122 --> 00:01:30.760
currentTime reads 0, 100 and 300, after a few real milliseconds.

23
00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:34.131
Only the test dispatcher's delays are virtual.

24
00:01:34.131 --> 00:01:38.809
A delay on Dispatchers.Default stays real, so inject the dispatcher.

25
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:40.926
This is Kotlin only.

26
00:01:40.926 --> 00:01:45.449
withLock around the whole sequence, and a test that reads currentTime.

27
00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:52.474
So a one-thread view keeps others out only between suspensions.

28
00:01:52.474 --> 00:01:54.306
A Mutex holds across them.

29
00:01:54.306 --> 00:01:57.760
And runTest jumps over delays on a virtual clock.

30
00:01:57.967 --> 00:01:58.738
Quick check.

31
00:01:58.738 --> 00:02:04.525
On a one-thread view, a credit reads and writes the balance with no suspension in between.

32
00:02:04.525 --> 00:02:06.904
Can two credits still lose an update?
